<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">An edge-weighted graph <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly with loops, is <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">antiferromagnetic</em> if its adjacency matrix is entrywise nonnegative and has at most one positive eigenvalue, counted with multiplicity. We show that, for any graph <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
    
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(G,H)\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">whenever <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is antiferromagnetic. In fact, we prove a vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening of this inequality, allowing a different fugacity vector at each vertex of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives a common generalization of the lower-bound inequalities of Sah, Sawhney, Stoner, and Zhao for independent sets, of Csikvári for <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-colorings, and of the authors for semiproper colorings with at most two proper colors. Furthermore, it confirms recent conjectures of the authors and of Davies and LeBlanc. A key ingredient, of independent interest, is a strengthening of the delete-one form of Shearer’s inequality for Lorentzian measures, which provides a new approach to graph homomorphism inequalities.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the fundamental reasons why complete graphs are important in graph theory is their repeated emergence as extremizers in counting problems on graphs. Throughout, let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite simple graph, and write <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(G)\coloneqq\lvert V(G)\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(G)\coloneqq\lvert V(G)\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, if <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i(G)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of independent sets of a <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, Cutler and Radcliffe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> proved that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i(G)^{1/v(G)}\geq i(K_{d+1})^{1/(d+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i(G)^{1/v(G)}\geq i(K_{d+1})^{1/(d+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, after the natural normalization that makes the quantity invariant under vertex-disjoint unions of copies of the same graph, the complete graph <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the number of independent sets. Csikvári’s theorem gives the analogous statement for the number of proper <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-colorings; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Further work in this direction includes results for the independence polynomial and arbitrary degree sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, partial results for the antiferromagnetic Ising model on cubic graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and results for semiproper colorings with a small number of proper colors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. These results may suggest a common principle, but their proofs are tailored to the individual models and, until now, no general criterion explained when complete graphs should be minimizers.
This leads to a fundamental question:</p>
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><em id="S1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">What structural condition on a counting model forces complete graphs to be minimizers?</em></p>
<p id="S1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We identify a broad class of models for which complete graphs are minimizers, specified by a simple spectral condition.
Let <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite nonempty set of spins. By a <em id="S1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model</em> on <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, we mean a symmetric matrix <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H=(H(\sigma,\tau):\sigma,\tau\in I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(H(\sigma,\tau):\sigma,\tau\in I)</annotation></semantics></math> with nonnegative entries.
Equivalently, a model <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-weighted graph on <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly with loops.
For a graph <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, its weighted homomorphism count into <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hom(G,H)\coloneqq\sum_{\phi\colon V(G)\to I}\prod_{uv\in E(G)}H(\phi(u),\phi(v))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)\coloneqq\sum_{\phi\colon V(G)\to I}\prod_{uv\in E(G)}H(\phi(u),\phi(v)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">When the edge weights of <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-valued, this is the usual number of graph homomorphisms from <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. A symmetric entrywise-nonnegative matrix is <em id="S1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">antiferromagnetic</em> if it has at most one positive eigenvalue, counted with multiplicity; a model <math id="S1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p1.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">antiferromagnetic</em> if its underlying matrix is antiferromagnetic.
Our main result is the following. For a graph <math id="S1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be an antiferromagnetic model. For every graph <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(G,H)\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)^{1/(d_{v}+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)^{1/(d_{v}+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following the terminology of Sah, Sawhney, Stoner, and Zhao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, we say that a model <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">clique-minimizing</em> if (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) holds for every graph <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> says that every antiferromagnetic model is clique-minimizing. In particular, if <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular, the conclusion becomes</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(G,H)^{1/v(G)}\geq\hom(K_{d+1},H)^{1/(d+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)^{1/v(G)}\geq\hom(K_{d+1},H)^{1/(d+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">while for an irregular graph the bound is a product of clique homomorphism counts determined by the individual vertex degrees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The class of antiferromagnetic models contains the principal examples motivating the problem. The hard-core model
<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}^{\circ}=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}^{\circ}=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>
encodes independent sets; the choice <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes proper <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-colorings; and, more generally, <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{q}^{\ell\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}^{\ell\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, the complete graph on <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> vertices with loops on exactly <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, encodes semiproper colorings. The antiferromagnetic Ising model with edge activity <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq B\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq B\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> has interaction matrix
<math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{B}=\begin{pmatrix}B&amp;1\\
1&amp;B\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi></mtd><mtd><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{B}=\begin{pmatrix}B&amp;1\\
1&amp;B\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each of these matrices has at most one positive eigenvalue. Consequently, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> recovers the known clique-minimization results for independent sets and proper colorings, extends the latter from regular graphs to arbitrary degree sequences, and establishes the corresponding inequality for all antiferromagnetic Ising models and (semi)proper colorings. More generally, it settles our conjecture that every antiferromagnetic model is clique-minimizing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Conjecture 1.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We in fact prove a stronger result in which every vertex of the source graph may have its own fugacity vector. Let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a model on a spin set <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of nonnegative reals. For each <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}=(\lambda^{(v)}_{\sigma}:\sigma\in I)\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}=(\lambda^{(v)}_{\sigma}:\sigma\in I)\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{I}</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector of vertex fugacities, and define the <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vertex-inhomogeneous partition function of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math></em> by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{Z}_{G}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}:v\in V(G))\coloneqq\sum_{{\bm{\sigma}}\in I^{V(G)}}\prod_{uv\in E(G)}H(\sigma_{u},\sigma_{v})\prod_{v\in V(G)}\lambda^{(v)}_{\sigma_{v}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝝈</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Z}_{G}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}:v\in V(G))\coloneqq\sum_{{\bm{\sigma}}\in I^{V(G)}}\prod_{uv\in E(G)}H(\sigma_{u},\sigma_{v})\prod_{v\in V(G)}\lambda^{(v)}_{\sigma_{v}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For a single fugacity vector <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the <em id="S1.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(vertex-homogeneous) partition function</em> by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{G}({\bm{\lambda}})\coloneqq\widetilde{Z}_{G}({\bm{\lambda}}:v\in V(G))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}({\bm{\lambda}})\coloneqq\widetilde{Z}_{G}({\bm{\lambda}}:v\in V(G)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{m}\coloneqq Z_{K_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{m}\coloneqq Z_{K_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <em id="S1.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">clique partition function</em>.
In particular, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\mathbf{1})=\hom(G,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\mathbf{1})=\hom(G,H)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the all-ones vector.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be an antiferromagnetic model. For every graph <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and every collection of fugacity vectors
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝛌</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{I}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{Z}_{G}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}:v\in V(G))\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}Z_{d_{v}+1}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})^{1/(d_{v}+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Z}_{G}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}:v\in V(G))\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}Z_{d_{v}+1}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})^{1/(d_{v}+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Taking <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> gives <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. Moreover, equality in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 1.2 ‣ Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) holds whenever <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of cliques and the fugacity vectors are constant on each connected component, so the inequality is sharp.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 1.2 ‣ Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) characterizes the class of antiferromagnetic models. Suppose that a model <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 1.2 ‣ Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) for every graph <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and every collection of fugacity vectors. Let <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and assign arbitrary fugacity vectors <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{I}</annotation></semantics></math> to its two vertices. Since <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is entrywise nonnegative, the resulting inequality is equivalent, after squaring, to</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x}^{\top}H\mathbf{y})^{2}\geq(\mathbf{x}^{\top}H\mathbf{x})(\mathbf{y}^{\top}H\mathbf{y})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x}^{\top}H\mathbf{y})^{2}\geq(\mathbf{x}^{\top}H\mathbf{x})(\mathbf{y}^{\top}H\mathbf{y}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">It follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 5.3]</cite> that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is antiferromagnetic.
By contrast, the clique-minimizing inequality in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) does not characterize the class of antiferromagnetic models; in <a href="#S6" title="6 Concluding remarks ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we will see examples of clique-minimizing models which are not antiferromagnetic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The vertex-inhomogeneous formulation also provides a variety of examples. It captures multivariate independence polynomials, weighted list-homomorphism and list-coloring problems, and spin systems with vertex-dependent external fields.
It is also the natural setting for one-vertex marginals: logarithmic differentiation with respect to the fugacity of a specified spin at a specified vertex recovers the corresponding marginal probability, a viewpoint central to the occupancy method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Related multivariate partition functions arise in Potts models with external fields <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and in zero-freeness and cluster-expansion methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As a consequence, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> yields results that do not follow from the homogeneous formulation alone.
In particular, it settles a conjecture from our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Conjecture 4.2]</cite>.
It also extends the vertex-inhomogeneous results for <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{3}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{3}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> from our earlier work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{q}^{\circ}\coloneqq K_{q}^{1\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}^{\circ}\coloneqq K_{q}^{1\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, which strengthen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Theorem 1.7]</cite> by replacing a common fugacity with arbitrary vertex-dependent fugacities.
Finally, the theorem confirms a conjecture of Davies and LeBlanc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Conjecture 5(ii)]</cite> on antiferromagnetic Ising models and regular source graphs, which they described as “somewhat bold”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We finally remark that separate fugacity parameters also help in our inductive argument on <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(G)</annotation></semantics></math> in the proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>. If a vertex <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is deleted after conditioning on its spin <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, then the fugacity vector at each neighbor <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in N(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in N(w)</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied coordinatewise by the <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-row of <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, a vertex-homogeneous problem immediately becomes vertex-inhomogeneous under the basic recurrence for the partition function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related graph homomorphism inequalities</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The lower bound in <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> fits into a broader extremal picture in which cliques and complete bipartite graphs provide complementary extremizers.
The classical extremal theory for independent sets begins with work of Alon and Kahn and culminates, in the regular case, in Zhao’s theorem that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{d,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d,d}</annotation></semantics></math> maximizes the normalized hard-core partition function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Galvin and Tetali extended Kahn’s entropy method to weighted homomorphism counts on bipartite regular graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. To extend the comparison to irregular graphs, Sah, Sawhney, Stoner, and Zhao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> introduced the following notion: a model <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">biclique-maximizing</em> if every graph <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> without isolated vertices satisfies</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(G,H)\leq\prod_{uv\in E(G)}\hom(K_{d_{u},d_{v}},H)^{1/(d_{u}d_{v})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)\leq\prod_{uv\in E(G)}\hom(K_{d_{u},d_{v}},H)^{1/(d_{u}d_{v})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">They proved this inequality for several broad classes and conjectured that every antiferromagnetic model is biclique-maximizing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Conjecture 1.16]</cite>. This conjecture remains open.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, in the same paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Theorem 1.14]</cite>, it is shown that every <em id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ferromagnetic</em> model, meaning every symmetric positive-semidefinite model, is <em id="S1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">clique-maximizing</em>:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(G,H)\leq\prod_{v\in V(G)}\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)^{1/(d_{v}+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)\leq\prod_{v\in V(G)}\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)^{1/(d_{v}+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, positive semidefiniteness forces the clique expression to be an upper bound, whereas <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> shows that antiferromagnetism forces the same expression to be a lower bound. For antiferromagnetic models, the expected global picture is therefore that cliques and bicliques provide the sharp lower and upper bounds, respectively. The present paper establishes the lower half of this picture in full generality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our related work with Oh <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, we used Lorentzian polynomials to study the biclique-maximizing conjecture from the perspective of the source graph. The present paper uses the same algebraic connection in a different direction: rather than proving an upper bound by comparison with bicliques, we extract concavity and entropy contraction properties of clique partition functions and use them to prove the lower bound.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Methodology</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a connection between Lorentzian polynomials and entropy contraction, yielding a new approach to graph homomorphism inequalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first ingredient is the Lorentzian property of clique partition functions. Introduced by Brändén and Huh <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Lorentzian polynomials provide a robust framework for log-concavity and negative-dependence phenomena. Their relevance here stems from the fact that, in degree two, the Hessian condition in the definition of Lorentzian polynomials coincides with the antiferromagnetic condition. In particular, <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> shows that every clique partition function of an entrywise-positive antiferromagnetic model is Lorentzian, a result that already appeared in our work with Oh <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Here, the resulting concavity provides the tangent-plane inequalities that serve as the starting point for our entropy argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second ingredient is a strengthening of the delete-one case of Shearer’s inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Equation (22)]</cite> for exchangeable Lorentzian laws. Here, a law on <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of letters, is exchangeable if it is invariant under coordinate permutations. The count vector of a word records the multiplicity of each letter, and we call a law Lorentzian if the multivariate probability generating function of its count vector is Lorentzian. At the level of generating polynomials, this notion may be viewed as a multiset analogue of the Lorentzian probability measures introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Section 4.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a random word <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(X_{1},\ldots,X_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(X_{1},\ldots,X_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> with an exchangeable Lorentzian law, we show in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 3.7. ‣ 3.2 Exchangeable Lorentzian laws and entropy contraction ‣ 3 Entropy inequalities for exchangeable Lorentzian laws ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{m}\Ent(X_{[m]\setminus j})\geq(m-2)\Ent(X)+\sum_{j=1}^{m}\Ent(X_{j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{m}\Ent(X_{[m]\setminus j})\geq(m-2)\Ent(X)+\sum_{j=1}^{m}\Ent(X_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed, subtracting the nonnegative “total correlation” <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{m}\Ent(X_{j})-\Ent(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{m}\Ent(X_{j})-\Ent(X)</annotation></semantics></math> from the lower bound gives the delete-one form of Shearer’s inequality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our main tool in proving this is a one-coordinate relative entropy contraction, which may be viewed as a multiset analogue of entropic independence due to Anari, Jain, Koehler, Pham, and Vuong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
In <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.2 Exchangeable Lorentzian laws and entropy contraction ‣ 3 Entropy inequalities for exchangeable Lorentzian laws ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, we show that if <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> are exchangeable laws on <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is Lorentzian, then</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mu_{1}\mathbin{\|}\nu_{1})\leq\frac{1}{m}D(\mu\mathbin{\|}\nu)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mu_{1}\mathbin{\|}\nu_{1})\leq\frac{1}{m}D(\mu\mathbin{\|}\nu),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are their one-coordinate marginals. Exchangeability identifies <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mu\mathbin{\|}\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mu\mathbin{\|}\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> with the relative entropy between the induced count laws, and the contraction follows from concavity of the <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th root of a Lorentzian generating polynomial and the variational formula for relative entropy. Although entropy inequalities have long been used in extremal graph theory, this use of relative entropy contraction appears to be new in the study of graph homomorphism inequalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Proof strategy</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After reducing to entrywise-positive models, we follow the standard inductive localization strategy used in our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> as well as others <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. First, we choose a vertex <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> of maximum degree <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{w}=\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{w}=\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, expand the vertex-inhomogeneous partition function according to the spin assigned to <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply the induction hypothesis to <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\setminus w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\setminus w</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, let <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=K_{2}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=K_{2}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}=(\lambda_{v},\mu_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}=(\lambda_{v},\mu_{v})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are the fugacities of the non-looped and looped vertices of <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Then <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})=\mu_{v}^{d}+d\lambda_{v}\mu_{v}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})=\mu_{v}^{d}+d\lambda_{v}\mu_{v}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using a recurrence relation between <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{Z}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Z}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{Z}_{G\setminus w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Z}_{G\setminus w}</annotation></semantics></math> that extends the standard recurrence relation for vertex-homogeneous partition functions</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda,\mu)=\mu Z_{G\setminus w}(\lambda,\mu)+\lambda\mu^{d_{w}}Z_{G\setminus(\{w\}\cup N(w))}(\lambda,\mu)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda,\mu)=\mu Z_{G\setminus w}(\lambda,\mu)+\lambda\mu^{d_{w}}Z_{G\setminus(\{w\}\cup N(w))}(\lambda,\mu),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">we see that the inductive strategy succeeds if we prove the inequality</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{w}\prod_{v\in N(w)}Z_{d_{v}}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}}}+\lambda_{w}\prod_{v\in N(w)}Z_{d_{v}}(0,\mu_{v})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}}}\geq Z_{\Delta+1}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(w)})^{\frac{1}{\Delta+1}}\prod_{v\in N(w)}Z_{d_{v}+1}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{w}\prod_{v\in N(w)}Z_{d_{v}}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}}}+\lambda_{w}\prod_{v\in N(w)}Z_{d_{v}}(0,\mu_{v})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}}}\geq Z_{\Delta+1}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(w)})^{\frac{1}{\Delta+1}}\prod_{v\in N(w)}Z_{d_{v}+1}({\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This slightly generalizes Equation (2.2) in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which treats the case <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{v}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{v}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Once we focus on variables <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S1.E4" title="Equation 1.4 ‣ 1.4 Proof strategy ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>) is simply of the form</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A\mu_{w}+B\lambda_{w}\geq(\mu_{w}^{\Delta+1}+(\Delta+1)\lambda_{w}\mu_{w}^{\Delta})^{\frac{1}{\Delta+1}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A\mu_{w}+B\lambda_{w}\geq(\mu_{w}^{\Delta+1}+(\Delta+1)\lambda_{w}\mu_{w}^{\Delta})^{\frac{1}{\Delta+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where the left-hand side is linear and the right-hand side is concave.
The first obstacle is that both <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are complicated products of rational powers of partition functions <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{v}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{v}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in N(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in N(w)</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, we resolved this issue by looking at the “dual set” <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, which characterizes tangent (hyper)planes of <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{\Delta+1})^{\frac{1}{\Delta+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{\Delta+1})^{\frac{1}{\Delta+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The log-convexity of <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> allowed us to handle the factors on each <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in N(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in N(w)</annotation></semantics></math> individually, reducing the inequality to the <em id="S1.SS4.p2.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local membership problem</em>, which asks whether a particular vector belongs to <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This framework successfully carries over to a more general setting using the theory of Lorentzian polynomials. It is not hard to check that the dual set is again log-convex for any model (<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5.1 Inductive localization and the dual set ‣ 5 Clique minimization for antiferromagnetic models ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>). Also, since <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lorentzian, <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{d})^{1/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{d})^{1/d}</annotation></semantics></math> is concave and therefore its tangent hyperplane lies above its graph. However, the local membership problem remains challenging. Even in our previous work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Lemma 3.2]</cite> illustrates that the problems may be complicated even for the specific case <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=K_{3}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=K_{3}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, we verified them by model-specific, low-dimensional calculations only for <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{3}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{3}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our main technical contribution, culminating in <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5.1 Inductive localization and the dual set ‣ 5 Clique minimization for antiferromagnetic models ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, solves this problem for general antiferromagnetic models by replacing the previous ad-hoc arguments with an abstract information-theoretic approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate, fix a neighbor <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> be its degree. In <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Inductive localization and the dual set ‣ 5 Clique minimization for antiferromagnetic models ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the desired local membership problem is shown to be equivalent to the statement that an auxiliary quantity, which we call the <em id="S1.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimized pressure</em>, is nonpositive (<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5.5. ‣ 5.2 Resolution of the local membership problem ‣ 5 Clique minimization for antiferromagnetic models ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>). Entropy inequalities for the associated Lorentzian laws, combined with the Gibbs variational formula, yield the monotonicity relation for the optimized pressure established in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4 Optimized pressure and its monotonicity ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
Concavity of <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{d+1})^{1/(d+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{d+1})^{1/(d+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> establishes nonpositivity at clique size <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 5.4. ‣ 5.2 Resolution of the local membership problem ‣ 5 Clique minimization for antiferromagnetic models ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>), and monotonicity extends this conclusion to every clique size up to <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
This concludes the proof of <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5.1 Inductive localization and the dual set ‣ 5 Clique minimization for antiferromagnetic models ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Organization</h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> fixes notation and collects preliminary lemmas. It also reviews Lorentzian polynomials and relates them to antiferromagnetic models via clique partition functions. <a href="#S3" title="3 Entropy inequalities for exchangeable Lorentzian laws ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> develops entropy inequalities for exchangeable Lorentzian laws. In <a href="#S4" title="4 Optimized pressure and its monotonicity ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we combine these results to derive a monotonicity property of the optimized pressure. We apply this abstract framework to prove <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> in <a href="#S5" title="5 Clique minimization for antiferromagnetic models ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Finally, <a href="#S6" title="6 Concluding remarks ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> concludes with further remarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this section, <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a finite index set. For a positive integer <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[m]\coloneqq\{1,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m]\coloneqq\{1,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of nonnegative real numbers; we also write <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> for the set of positive reals and <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for the set of nonnegative integers. For a function <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> of variables <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{\sigma})_{\sigma\in J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{\sigma})_{\sigma\in J}</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\sigma}f\coloneqq\partial_{x_{\sigma}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\sigma}f\coloneqq\partial_{x_{\sigma}}f</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the all-ones vector of the relevant dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We generally use boldface letters for vectors and corresponding ordinary letters for their entries. For example, if <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a set and <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in A^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>J</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in A^{J}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{i}:i\in J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{i}:i\in J)</annotation></semantics></math>. We identify <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with the function <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\to A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\mapsto x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\mapsto x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>J</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{J}</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}\odot\mathbf{y}\coloneqq(x_{i}y_{i}:i\in J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\odot\mathbf{y}\coloneqq(x_{i}y_{i}:i\in J)</annotation></semantics></math> for the coordinatewise product. For <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\alpha}}\in(\mathbb{N}_{0})^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>J</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\alpha}}\in(\mathbb{N}_{0})^{J}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{{\bm{\alpha}}}\coloneqq\prod_{i\in J}x_{i}^{\alpha_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{{\bm{\alpha}}}\coloneqq\prod_{i\in J}x_{i}^{\alpha_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following is an elementary result for homogeneous functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Euler’s homogeneous function theorem)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be a real-valued function on an open cone <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>J</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{J}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable and homogeneous of degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t\mathbf{x})=t^{k}f(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t\mathbf{x})=t^{k}f(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in J}x_{i}\partial_{i}f(\mathbf{x})=kf(\mathbf{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in J}x_{i}\partial_{i}f(\mathbf{x})=kf(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in C</annotation></semantics></math> and differentiate the identity <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t\mathbf{x})=t^{k}f(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t\mathbf{x})=t^{k}f(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We will use the following form of the Perron–Frobenius theorem; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Section 7]</cite>.
Let <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=(B_{ij})_{i,j\in J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=(B_{ij})_{i,j\in J}</annotation></semantics></math> be a square matrix with nonnegative entries.
We say <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reducible</em> if there exists a permutation matrix <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PBP^{\top}=\begin{pmatrix}X&amp;Y\\
0&amp;Z\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>Y</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>Z</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PBP^{\top}=\begin{pmatrix}X&amp;Y\\
0&amp;Z\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> are square matrices of order at least <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. If no such permutation matrix exists, <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">irreducible</em>. If <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric, then there is a permutation matrix <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PBP^{\top}=\begin{pmatrix}B_{1}&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
0&amp;B_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{r}\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PBP^{\top}=\begin{pmatrix}B_{1}&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
0&amp;B_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{r}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where each diagonal block <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.
We call the matrices <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">irreducible blocks</em> of <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Perron–Frobenius)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonzero irreducible square matrix with nonnegative entries. Then its spectral radius <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(B)</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive eigenvalue of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> has a corresponding entrywise-positive eigenvector.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be an antiferromagnetic matrix with no zero rows. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric, the decomposition above is block diagonal. Because <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> has no zero rows, every irreducible block of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 (Perron–Frobenius). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, each irreducible block of <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> has at least one positive eigenvalue, which is also an eigenvalue of <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is antiferromagnetic, it has only one irreducible block and is therefore irreducible.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the recursive definition of Lorentzian polynomials from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite nonempty index set. For a real polynomial <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in the variables <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, its <em id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hessian</em> <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix whose rows and columns are indexed by <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and whose <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>-entry is <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\sigma}\partial_{\tau}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\sigma}\partial_{\tau}f</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">support</em> of <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\alpha}}\in(\mathbb{N}_{0})^{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\alpha}}\in(\mathbb{N}_{0})^{I}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{{\bm{\alpha}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{{\bm{\alpha}}}</annotation></semantics></math> appears in <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with nonzero coefficient. A set <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq(\mathbb{N}_{0})^{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq(\mathbb{N}_{0})^{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><span id="S2.p7.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">M</span>-convex</em> if, for every <math id="S2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\alpha}},{\bm{\beta}}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\alpha}},{\bm{\beta}}\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, whenever <math id="S2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\sigma}&gt;\beta_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\sigma}&gt;\beta_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in I</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\tau}&lt;\beta_{\tau},\qquad{\bm{\alpha}}-\mathbf{e}_{\sigma}+\mathbf{e}_{\tau}\in S,\qquad{\bm{\beta}}-\mathbf{e}_{\tau}+\mathbf{e}_{\sigma}\in S," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\tau}&lt;\beta_{\tau},\qquad{\bm{\alpha}}-\mathbf{e}_{\sigma}+\mathbf{e}_{\tau}\in S,\qquad{\bm{\beta}}-\mathbf{e}_{\tau}+\mathbf{e}_{\sigma}\in S,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard unit vector whose <math id="S2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate is <math id="S2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and whose other coordinates are <math id="S2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
A polynomial <math id="S2.p7.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f\in\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0}[x_{\sigma}:\sigma\in I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0}[x_{\sigma}:\sigma\in I]</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S2.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lorentzian</em> if either <math id="S2.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant or <math id="S2.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and the following conditions hold:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is homogeneous and has M-convex support;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f</annotation></semantics></math> is antiferromagnetic;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\sigma}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\sigma}f</annotation></semantics></math> is either zero or Lorentzian.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a Lorentzian polynomial of degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{1/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{1/d}</annotation></semantics></math> is concave on the positive orthant.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math>, this is immediate because <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is linear. For <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, this follows from Theorem 2.30 and Proposition 2.33 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-variable Lorentzian polynomial of degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the following polynomials are also Lorentzian:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cf(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cf(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for a positive scalar </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{a}\odot\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{a}\odot\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathbf{b}}f(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_{i}\partial_{i}f(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>𝐛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathbf{b}}f(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_{i}\partial_{i}f(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+}_{0})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first assertion is immediate from the definition, and the remaining assertions follow from Theorem 2.10 and Corollary 2.11 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We need an auxiliary lemma on derivatives of clique partition functions, which is useful in connecting the theory of Lorentzian polynomials to our setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be any model on a spin set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be its clique partition function.
For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+})^{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in(\mathbb{R}^{+})^{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in I</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
