<p id="S6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">so <math id="S6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is clique-minimizing. For example, <math id="S6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}^{\circ}\otimes K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}^{\circ}\otimes K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is clique-minimizing but not antiferromagnetic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another way to construct examples is to consider models <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(K_{r},H)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(K_{r},H)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, it suffices to verify (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) for every <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with maximum degree at most <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\geq r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\geq r-1</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(K_{d_{v}+1},H)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so the right-hand side of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) vanishes. Both sides of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) factor over connected components of <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, so one may restrict further to connected <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=3</annotation></semantics></math>, every connected graph <math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with maximum degree at most <math id="S6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and equality holds in both cases, so (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) holds for any bipartite graph <math id="S6.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, not necessarily antiferromagnetic.
When <math id="S6.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>, it remains to check connected graphs <math id="S6.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(G)\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(G)\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, which are paths and cycles. One may check that the model <math id="S6.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> given by a triangle with one pendant edge is clique-minimizing but not antiferromagnetic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It would also be interesting to understand equality and stability in the vertex-inhomogeneous inequality (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 1.2 ‣ Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1 A vertex-inhomogeneous strengthening ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>).
As mentioned, disjoint unions of cliques with the fugacity vectors satisfying <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\lambda}}^{(u)}={\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\lambda}}^{(u)}={\bm{\lambda}}^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> attain equality. For a fixed antiferromagnetic model, one may ask whether these are the only extremizers under suitable nondegeneracy assumptions and whether near equality forces the source graph <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to be close to a disjoint union of cliques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We note that it was conjectured in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, Conjecture 7.1]</cite> that, for a connected graph <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the vertex-homogeneous partition function <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is Lorentzian for every antiferromagnetic model <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a clique. Thus, this conjecture predicts that the Lorentzian property established in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Antiferromagnetic models are clique-minimizing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> characterizes cliques among connected source graphs.</p>
</div>
